<clickhouse>
  <!-- SERVER-WIDE MEMORY CEILING.

       The default ratio is 0.9 of host RAM (~14 GB on the 16 GB droplet). But
       ClickHouse shares the host with Kafka (4 GB limit), the Trench API and both
       adapters, so the kernel runs out of memory before ClickHouse's own limit
       is reached. The kernel then kills clickhouse-server outright instead of
       ClickHouse cancelling one query with MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED.

       2026-11-18 13:09 UTC: two ad-hoc analytics queries (a window function
       over a week of page views) pushed RSS to 12.6 GB, the global OOM killer
       took ClickHouse down, the API answered 500 for ~1 minute (it checks
       api_keys in ClickHouse on every request) and adapters dropped ~3.8k events.

       0.6 of 16 GB is ~9.4 GB: normal load sits around 2.5 GB, which leaves room
       for merges and per-user query limits while keeping the host out of OOM.

       HOW TO APPLY: this file is a new bind mount, so the container must be
       recreated, not just restarted (see metabase-user.xml for why a plain
       `up -d` is not enough):

           docker compose up -d clickhouse    (with the force-recreate flag)

       Later edits to the value are picked up by config reload, but git pull
       replaces the file inode, so recreate after every change anyway.

       Verify:
           SELECT formatReadableSize(value::UInt64) FROM system.server_settings
           WHERE name = 'max_server_memory_usage'                     -->
  <max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>0.6</max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>
</clickhouse>
